<svelte:head><title>Research methods · Voynich@home</title><meta name="description" content="See how Voynich@home defines searches, tests known messages and controls, checks submitted results, and reviews scientific claims."/></svelte:head>
<div class="page narrow prose"><p class="eyebrow">Research methods</p><h1>From a search result to evidence</h1><p class="lede">Before computation begins, a campaign fixes the search space, data, budget, comparisons, and decision rule. Another researcher can repeat the work from the complete record.</p>
<h2>Known-message recovery</h2><p>Latin and Italian provide a practical starting scope; their inclusion makes no claim about the manuscript’s language. The search uses compact character-sequence models trained on separate source works. Reports keep the decoder’s output unchanged rather than rewriting it as fluent prose.</p><p>The program compares ordinary substitution, homophonic substitution, and documented encodings that expand letters into groups. It tests 1,000, 5,000, and 20,000 normalized characters. For methods that support restarts, it compares 1, 8, and 64 starts.</p><p>The completed development panel selected frozen settings. The current evaluation is testing 100 fresh message-and-key cases per reported condition, with the same search budgets applied to shuffled and generated controls. That evaluation is still running and has no published report. The development observations therefore remain development evidence.</p>
<h2>Faithful encoding tests</h2><p>Naibbe is a documented encoding with several Voynich-like properties, though its author does not claim it solves the manuscript. The implementation preserves the published tables and notices and rejects ambiguous output. The project labels its key-randomized extension separately. After the decoder parses the known symbol-group structure, that extension reduces to substitution, so recovering it does not demonstrate a solution for unknown tables or unrestricted verbose ciphers.</p><p><a href="https://www.michaelgreshko.com/naibbe-cipher">Read Michael Greshko’s Naibbe explanation</a> · <a href="https://github.com/javidmardanov/VoynichAtHome/tree/33debe761229e99816cd6d0f1efc1bc65c775b4d/third_party/naibbe">Review the imported implementation and terms</a></p>
<h2>Requirements for a manuscript campaign</h2><p>Recovery tests must establish a useful operating range before a manuscript campaign can open. The campaign must declare its exact transcription, included passages, grouping and spacing rules, language resources, algorithm, seeds, budget, comparisons, and stopping conditions.</p><p>The record keeps pages, lines, paragraphs, uncertainty, and excluded material visible. Researchers study major manuscript text groups separately before combining them. Any candidate must include its mapping and unchanged decoder output, including unparsed symbols and exceptions.</p>
<h2 id="validation">Execution checks and scientific review</h2><p>The server gives the same task to more than one participant and performs a trusted replay before awarding credit. The replay checks whether the submitted output and score match the declared computation. Disagreements receive no credit. Interrupted tasks count as operating failures and carry no scientific meaning.</p><p>Browser identifiers cannot establish that results came from separate people or machines. A matching replay confirms the computation under the recorded conditions. A historical reading requires other evidence and specialist review.</p><p>Credit uses a fixed work estimate: the ceiling of search evaluations × normalized ciphertext symbols ÷ 1,000. Reported runtime and scientific interpretation do not change it. Team totals count checked credit from current members who publish their profiles.</p>
<h2 id="corrections">Scientific corrections</h2><p>The former “Rule C” did not justify its claimed 1% error guarantee. The project removed that claim, corrected inconsistent replicate counts and metrics, and stopped treating an empty adaptive search as evidence against an entire continuous parameter domain.</p><p>Earlier development work used the whole manuscript, so current manuscript results remain exploratory. A formal test of a fully specified generator calibrates fresh simulated target texts with the same declared procedure and records its assumptions and finite simulation limits.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/javidmardanov/VoynichAtHome/blob/33debe761229e99816cd6d0f1efc1bc65c775b4d/docs/research/acceptance-rule-review.md">Read the acceptance-rule review</a> · <a href="https://github.com/javidmardanov/VoynichAtHome/blob/33debe761229e99816cd6d0f1efc1bc65c775b4d/docs/SCIENTIFIC-CORRECTIONS.md">Read the corrections and interpretation</a></p>
<h2>Propose an experiment</h2><p>The human project owner decides which campaigns and releases to publish. The project controls the current development checks; outside researchers can add independent reproduction and subject expertise.</p><p>A proposal must include:</p><ul><li>A concrete research question.</li><li>Data sources, rights, and prior exposure.</li><li>A reproducible method and fixed computation budget.</li><li>Positive, negative, and generated comparisons.</li><li>A decision rule and stopping condition.</li><li>A plan to publish failures and successes.</li></ul><a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/javidmardanov/VoynichAtHome/issues/new?template=experiment-proposal.yml">Propose an experiment ↗</a>
</div>
